cfdTools - shell functions for setting up and running OpenFOAM parameter studies

Usage:

    . /path/to/cfdTools

  Put that line in ~/.bashrc to have the functions and the dictator tab completion
  available in every terminal. cfdTools must be sourced from bash: it uses bash arrays,
  which other shells index differently, so sourcing it from zsh or sh is refused rather
  than silently misbehaving. No OpenFOAM installation is required.

Contents:

    parmScanner       generate, and optionally run, a matrix of cases from a base case
    dictator          inspect and set dictionary parameters, with tab completion
    setDictEntry      set the value of one dictionary entry
    getDictEntry      print the value of one dictionary entry
    listDictEntries   print every entry path in a dictionary file
    columnAlign       align the columns of a parameter file for readability

  "parmScanner -help" prints this file.


================================================================================
parmScanner
================================================================================

Usage: parmScanner [OPTIONS]

Description:
  Copies a base case once per row of a parameter file, sets the parameters of each copy,
  and optionally runs a script in each of them.

Options:
  -file <parmFile>      Specify the name of the file containing the list of cases to generate.
  -exec <case_script>   Specify the script to be executed in each case after creation.
  -parallel             Run cases in parallel (background). Default: sequential execution.
  -maxJobs <N>          Maximum number of cases to run simultaneously (only with -parallel).
  -help                 Display this help message and exit.

Example of usage only creating cases (not running them):

  parmScanner -file parmFile.csv

Example of usage creating and running cases in one go:

  parmScanner -file parmFile.csv -exec Allrun -parallel -maxJobs 4

Example of parmFile.csv format:

    scanName                      myParmScan
    baseCase                      myBaseCase
    water.nu                      ddtSchemes.default          nx
    constant/transportProperties  system/fvSchemes            system/blockMeshDict
    1e00                          Euler                       20
    1e-01                         "CrankNicolson      0.9"    20
    1e-02                         "CrankNicolson      0.7"    40
    1e-03                         "CrankNicolson      0.6"    40
    1e-04                         "CrankNicolson      0.5"    80

  Line 1 names the scan, and hence the directory the cases are placed in.
  Line 2 names the base case to copy.
  Line 3 lists the parameters to vary, as full entry paths (see setDictEntry below).
  Line 4 gives the file each parameter lives in, relative to the case directory.
  Each following line is one case. Use double quotes for values containing spaces.

Notes:
  If a scan is named myScan, cases are placed in myScan/0000, myScan/0001, myScan/0002, ...

  A case is run at most once. When -exec is used, parmScanner creates log.<execScript> in
  the case as the script is launched, and skips any case in which that file already exists.
  A scan can therefore safely be re-run to add new cases: existing cases are left untouched,
  whether they finished, are still running or crashed. To re-run a case, delete its
  log.<execScript>.

  Parameters are set with setDictEntry, so a case that cannot be given the requested value
  aborts the scan rather than silently keeping the base case value.

  The scan can be deleted by executing:

    rm -rf myScan

  For a numbered list of all cases and their parameter values execute:

    cat myScan/parmScannerParms

  If libreoffice is installed, conveniently edit parmFile.csv with the command

    libreoffice --calc parmFile.csv

  In the libreoffice import dialog choose "Space" under "Separated by" and choose
  "Merge delimiters". Use columnAlign afterwards to tidy the column widths.


================================================================================
dictator, setDictEntry, getDictEntry, listDictEntries
================================================================================

Usage: dictator         <file> [<entry.path> [<value>]]
       setDictEntry     <file> <entry.path> <value>
       getDictEntry     <file> <entry.path>
       listDictEntries  <file>

Description:
  setDictEntry sets a single entry in a dictionary file. Only the value is rewritten; every
  other byte of the file is left untouched, so comments, formatting, blank lines and full
  floating point precision are all preserved. getDictEntry prints one entry's value and
  listDictEntries prints every entry path in a file. All three share one parser, which reads
  the dictionary as a character stream, so entries and sub-dicts may be split across lines
  or packed onto one, separated by spaces, tabs or nothing at all.

  dictator is the interactive front end, with tab completion at every position:

    dictator sys[TAB]                            -> system/
    dictator system/cont[TAB]                    -> system/controlDict
    dictator system/controlDict [TAB][TAB]       -> lists every parameter path
    dictator system/controlDict endT[TAB]        -> endTime
    dictator system/controlDict endTime [TAB]    -> fills in the current value
    dictator system/controlDict endTime 12       -> sets it to 12

  With two arguments dictator prints a value, with three it sets one.

  Entries are addressed by their full path from the top of the file, with sub-dicts
  separated by "." :

    setDictEntry system/blockMeshDict nz 160
    setDictEntry system/fvSchemes ddtSchemes.default "CrankNicolson 0.9"
    setDictEntry system/fvSolution 'solvers."alpha.water.*".nAlphaSubCycles' 4

  The path must match exactly one entry. If it matches none, or several, nothing is written,
  the file is left unchanged and a non-zero status is returned:

    $ setDictEntry system/fvSchemes default Euler
    "default" not found
        in file: system/fvSchemes

  A name is not searched for in the sub-dicts below it: "default" above names a top-level
  entry, not ddtSchemes.default. Tab completion is the way to find the path wanted.

  Entries appearing inside // or /* */ comments are ignored, as are #include and other
  #directive lines.

  dictator adds a parameter that does not exist yet, placing it after the last entry of
  the dictionary named in the path and indenting it to match:

    dictator system/controlDict.run tis 5      ->  Added tis = 5
    dictator system/controlDict.run endTime 12 ->  Set   endTime = 12

  The path is taken at face value, so "dictator constant/transportProperties rho 10" adds
  a top-level rho even though water.rho and air.rho exist. Check that a new entry landed
  where you intended; the Added/Set distinction in the message is there to make an
  unintended addition, such as a mistyped name, easy to spot. The dictionary named in the
  path must already exist: "dictator file noSuchDict.x 1" is refused.

  setDictEntry does NOT add by default, and reports a missing entry as an error instead.
  This is what parmScanner relies on, so that a mistyped parameter name in a parmFile
  aborts the scan rather than quietly adding a useless entry to every case. Pass -add to
  setDictEntry for dictator's behaviour; it returns 2 when it added and 0 when it replaced.

  Removing entries is not supported.

  The dictionary dialect is selected by the variable dictSyntax. Only "openfoam" (the
  default) is currently defined: entries "key value;" and sub-dicts "name { ... }". The
  dialect is described by six tokens (entry terminator, sub-dict open and close, line
  comment, block comment open and close), so another brace-and-terminator format is a
  one-line addition. Formats that express nesting through indentation, such as YAML, do
  not fit this model.


================================================================================
columnAlign
================================================================================

Usage: columnAlign <filename>

Description:
  Pads the columns of a white space separated file so they line up, and rewrites the file
  in place. Intended for keeping a parmFile.csv readable after rows have been added or
  edited.

  Known limitation: does not respect double quotation, so values containing spaces are
  treated as several columns.


